CertHub.register({"id":"sc-500","vendor":"Microsoft","name":"Microsoft Certified: Cloud and AI Security Engineer Associate (replaces Azure Security Engineer Associate / AZ-500)","short":"SC-500","exam":"SC-500","blurb":"Hands-on Azure security engineering for admins and engineers: identity and governance, storage, database and network protection, securing VMs, containers, app platforms and AI workloads, and running posture and threat operations with Defender for Cloud, Sentinel and Security Copilot.","status":"check","statusNote":"AZ-500 and the Azure Security Engineer Associate certification retired on Aug 31, 2026, so this plan (kept under the az-500 id) follows the replacement exam SC-500: Implementing End-to-End Security Controls for Cloud and AI Workloads (beta May 2026, generally available July 2026). Skills measured, as reported from the Microsoft Learn SC-500 study guide on Sept 24, 2026: identity, access and governance 20–25%; storage, databases and networking 25–30%; compute 20–25%; security posture 20–25%. Midpoints (22.5/27.5/22.5/22.5 = 95) were scaled to 100 and rounded to 24/28/24/24. Learn pages could not be opened directly from this environment (the ranges were read from search excerpts of the official page), so re-check the study guide, question count and exam length before booking.","lastVerified":"2026-09-24","notices":[{"from":"2026-09-24","until":"2027-03-31","text":"Exam AZ-500 and the Azure Security Engineer Associate certification retired on Aug 31, 2026 and can no longer be booked or renewed. This plan follows the replacement, SC-500 (Cloud and AI Security Engineer Associate). There is no conversion path: earned AZ-500 certifications stay on your transcript but you must pass SC-500 for the new credential."}],"examInfo":{"questions":"Not published (Microsoft associate exams are usually 40–60 questions, some case studies)","minutes":100,"pass":"700 on a 1–1000 scale","extra":"Seat time is about 120 minutes including agreements and survey; confirm the exam length on the SC-500 exam page."},"examSim":{"questions":50,"minutes":100},"sources":[{"label":"Microsoft Learn: SC-500 study guide (skills measured)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/sc-500"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: AZ-500 study guide (retired exam)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/resources/study-guides/az-500"},{"label":"Microsoft Learn: SC-500T00 course","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/courses/sc-500t00"}],"planWeeks":10,"hoursPerWeek":"8–10","domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Manage identity, access, and governance","w":24,"topics":["Microsoft Entra built-in roles vs Azure RBAC roles, scopes (management group, subscription, resource group, resource) and least privilege","Custom Azure RBAC roles: actions, notActions, dataActions and assignable scopes","Privileged Identity Management: eligible vs active assignments, activation with MFA, approval and justification, access reviews","Conditional Access: users and workload identities, cloud apps, conditions (sign-in risk, locations, device platform), grant and session controls, report-only mode","MFA and authentication methods policy, phishing-resistant methods (FIDO2, passkeys, Windows Hello), authentication strengths","Managed identities: system-assigned vs user-assigned, and replacing stored secrets with token-based access","App registrations vs enterprise applications (service principals), API permissions, admin consent and user consent settings","Azure Key Vault: RBAC vs access policies, soft delete and purge protection, key rotation, network restrictions, Defender for Key Vault","Azure Policy: built-in vs custom definitions, initiatives, effects (Deny, Audit, Modify, DeployIfNotExists), remediation tasks, exemptions","Resource locks (CanNotDelete, ReadOnly), management group hierarchy and governance at scale","Finding and fixing over-privileged access with access reviews and Entra ID Protection risk policies"],"notes":["Skills measured: Manage identity, access, and governance (20–25%)","PIM, ID Protection and risk-based Conditional Access need Microsoft Entra ID P2 (a free trial exists)."],"labs":["Free Azure account: create a resource group, assign a user Reader at the resource group scope and Contributor on one storage account, then use Access control (IAM) > Check access to confirm the effective permissions. Cost: free.","Start a Microsoft Entra ID P2 (or Microsoft 365 E5) trial in a test tenant, make a user eligible for the Security Reader role in PIM with MFA and justification on activation, activate it and review the audit log. Cost warning: trials convert to paid unless cancelled; set a calendar reminder.","Create a Key Vault with RBAC permissions and purge protection, give a VM's system-assigned managed identity Key Vault Secrets User, read a secret from the VM with `curl` to the IMDS token endpoint, then assign the built-in policy 'Key vaults should have soft delete enabled' in Audit mode. Cost warning: stop and delete the VM and resource group when done."]},{"id":2,"name":"Secure storage, databases, and networking","w":28,"topics":["Storage authorization: Entra ID with data-plane RBAC, account keys, disabling Shared Key, key rotation","Shared access signatures: user delegation vs service vs account SAS, stored access policies and revocation","Storage encryption: Microsoft-managed vs customer-managed keys, infrastructure encryption, immutable blob storage","Storage firewall, trusted services exceptions, and Defender for Storage (activity monitoring, malware scanning, sensitive data threat detection)","Azure SQL security: Entra-only authentication, server and database firewall rules, TDE with CMK, Always Encrypted, dynamic data masking, auditing, Defender for SQL","Network security groups and application security groups, service tags, rule priority and default rules","Azure Virtual Network Manager security admin rules vs NSGs","Private endpoints and Private Link vs service endpoints, private DNS zones","Azure Firewall (Standard vs Premium: TLS inspection, IDPS, URL filtering), Firewall Manager and firewall policy, forced tunneling with user-defined routes","Web Application Firewall on Application Gateway and Front Door, DDoS Protection","Site-to-site and point-to-site VPN, Virtual WAN secured hubs, Microsoft Entra Private Access (ZTNA)","Network Watcher: IP flow verify, effective security rules, VNet flow logs and traffic analytics"],"notes":["Skills measured: Secure storage, databases, and networking (25–30%) — the heaviest domain"],"labs":["Free Azure account: create a storage account, disable 'Allow storage account key access', give yourself Storage Blob Data Contributor and upload a blob with `az storage blob upload --auth-mode login`; then create a user delegation SAS with a 1-hour expiry and test it in a private browser window. Cost: cents; delete the account afterwards.","Build a VNet with web and data subnets, create ASGs 'web' and 'db', write NSG rules allowing only web->db on 1433, and prove the result with Network Watcher IP flow verify and effective security rules. Cost warning: use B-series VMs, deallocate them immediately, and delete the resource group.","Create an Azure SQL Database on the free offer, switch the server to Microsoft Entra-only authentication, add a private endpoint with a privatelink.database.windows.net DNS zone, enable auditing to a Log Analytics workspace and apply dynamic data masking to an email column. Cost warning: do not enable Azure Firewall or Bastion Standard for this lab; they bill hourly even when idle."]},{"id":3,"name":"Secure compute","w":24,"topics":["Security for AI: Defender for AI services threat protection, prompt injection and jailbreak alerts, Azure AI Content Safety Prompt Shields","Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI to find data overexposure to Microsoft 365 Copilot and AI apps","AI Gateway in Azure API Management in front of Microsoft Foundry models: managed identity authentication, token limits, logging","Microsoft Entra Agent ID: agent identities, Conditional Access and access management for AI agents","VM disk protection: encryption at host, Azure Disk Encryption, server-side encryption with customer-managed keys","Trusted launch: secure boot, vTPM and boot integrity monitoring","Azure Bastion and just-in-time VM access instead of public RDP/SSH","Defender for Servers (Plan 1 vs Plan 2), vulnerability assessment, Azure Arc for hybrid and multicloud servers, Azure Update Manager","AKS security: Entra ID integration, Azure RBAC for Kubernetes, private clusters, network policies, Defender for Containers, Azure Policy for AKS","Container registry security: disable the admin user, RBAC pull/push roles, private endpoints, image vulnerability scanning","App Service, Functions and Logic Apps: managed identities, Key Vault references, access restrictions, HTTPS only and minimum TLS, authentication (Easy Auth)"],"notes":["Skills measured: Secure compute (20–25%): security for AI; servers and virtual machines; application platform services"],"labs":["Free Azure account: deploy a small Linux VM with Trusted launch and encryption at host and no public IP, then connect through Azure Bastion Developer (free SKU where available). Cost warning: Bastion Basic/Standard bill hourly; deallocate the VM and delete the resource group the same day.","Deploy an App Service web app on the Free (F1) tier, enable a system-assigned managed identity, add a Key Vault reference in an app setting, turn on HTTPS only with minimum TLS 1.2 and add an access restriction that allows only your IP. Cost: free tier; delete when done.","Create an Azure OpenAI or Foundry model deployment, put it behind an API Management (Consumption tier) API that authenticates with its managed identity and applies a token-limit policy, then turn on the Defender for AI services 30-day trial and review any alerts. Cost warning: model tokens and APIM calls are billed; set a budget alert first and disable the Defender plan before the trial ends."]},{"id":4,"name":"Manage and monitor security posture","w":24,"topics":["Defender for Cloud: foundational CSPM vs Defender CSPM, secure score and recommendations","Defender CSPM features: attack path analysis, cloud security explorer, agentless scanning, governance rules","Regulatory compliance dashboard: Microsoft cloud security benchmark and adding standards","Workload protection plans (Servers, Storage, SQL, Containers, Key Vault, App Service, AI) and alert handling","Multicloud connectors for AWS and GCP, and workflow automation with Logic Apps","Microsoft Sentinel workspace design, data connectors, Azure Monitor Agent with data collection rules, Syslog and CEF, Windows security events","Sentinel analytics rules: scheduled, near-real-time, Microsoft security (incident creation) and anomaly rules; entity mapping","Automation rules vs playbooks, incident management, workbooks, hunting queries, watchlists and threat intelligence","Log tiers and retention (Analytics vs data lake/auxiliary), custom tables, KQL basics","Microsoft Security Copilot: capacity in SCUs, roles, plugins, promptbooks, standalone vs embedded experiences and agents","Microsoft Purview Audit for investigations"],"notes":["Skills measured: Manage and monitor security posture (20–25%)"],"labs":["Free Azure account: open Defender for Cloud, review your secure score and the Microsoft cloud security benchmark, remediate two recommendations (for example storage secure transfer and Key Vault purge protection) and watch the score change within 24 hours. Cost: foundational CSPM is free; do not enable paid plans you do not need.","Create a Log Analytics workspace, enable Microsoft Sentinel (check the free trial terms), connect the Microsoft Entra ID and Azure Activity connectors, and write a scheduled analytics rule in KQL that alerts when a role assignment is created. Cost warning: ingestion is billed per GB after the trial; limit connectors and set a daily cap.","Build an automation rule that assigns high-severity incidents to you, then a Logic Apps playbook that posts incident details to a Teams channel or email; trigger it from a test incident. Cost: Logic Apps consumption runs cost fractions of a cent; delete the workspace when finished."]}],"study":{"1":[["What is the difference between an eligible and an active assignment in PIM?","An active assignment gives the role permanently or for a set window with no action needed. An eligible assignment lets the user activate the role on demand for a limited time, optionally with MFA, justification, a ticket number or approval, so standing privilege is avoided."],["When would you choose a user-assigned managed identity over a system-assigned one?","When several resources need the same identity and permissions, or when the identity must survive the resource being deleted and recreated. System-assigned identities are tied one-to-one to a resource and are deleted with it."],["Explain the difference between an app registration and an enterprise application.","The app registration is the global application object defined in its home tenant (credentials, redirect URIs, requested permissions). The enterprise application is the service principal in each tenant where the app is used; it holds assignments, consent and sign-in settings for that tenant."],["Why use Key Vault's RBAC permission model instead of access policies?","RBAC gives per-secret/key/certificate scoping, uses the same Azure role assignments, supports PIM and Conditional Access on management, and separates who can manage the vault from who can read its contents. Access policies are vault-wide and anyone with Contributor on the vault can grant themselves access."],["Compare the Azure Policy effects Audit, Deny, Modify and DeployIfNotExists.","Audit logs non-compliance only. Deny blocks the create/update request. Modify adds or changes properties such as tags during create/update and via remediation. DeployIfNotExists deploys a related resource (for example a diagnostic setting) when it is missing, which needs a managed identity for remediation."],["What does a CanNotDelete lock do, and who does it apply to?","It lets authorized users read and modify a resource but blocks deletion. Locks apply to all users and roles, including Owners, until someone with Microsoft.Authorization/locks permissions removes the lock."]],"2":[["Why is a user delegation SAS preferred over a service SAS?","It is signed with a user delegation key obtained through Microsoft Entra credentials rather than the storage account key, so access is tied to an identity with RBAC, is auditable, and the account keys can be disabled."],["How do you revoke a service SAS before it expires?","Link it to a stored access policy and then change or delete that policy, or rotate the account key used to sign it (which invalidates every SAS signed with that key)."],["Private endpoint vs service endpoint: what is the key difference?","A private endpoint gives the PaaS resource a private IP in your VNet and can be reached from peered networks and on-premises; public access can be disabled. A service endpoint keeps the public endpoint but lets the service recognize traffic from a specific subnet over the Azure backbone."],["How do NSG rule priorities work?","Rules are processed from the lowest number (100) to the highest (4096) and processing stops at the first match. Default rules at 65000+ allow VNet and load balancer traffic and deny everything else inbound."],["Compare TDE, Always Encrypted and dynamic data masking for Azure SQL.","TDE encrypts data files and backups at rest transparently. Always Encrypted encrypts selected columns on the client so the database engine and DBAs never see plaintext. Dynamic data masking only hides values in query results for non-privileged users and is not encryption."],["What does Azure Firewall Premium add over Standard?","TLS inspection, signature-based IDPS, URL filtering on full paths, and web categories, which require decrypting outbound HTTPS with a certificate kept in Key Vault."]],"3":[["What does just-in-time VM access actually change?","Defender for Servers (Plan 2) adds NSG/Azure Firewall deny rules for management ports; when a user requests access, a temporary allow rule is created for the requested source IP and time window, then removed."],["Encryption at host vs Azure Disk Encryption: how do they differ?","Encryption at host encrypts temp disks and disk caches at the host with platform or customer-managed keys, with no in-guest agent. Azure Disk Encryption uses BitLocker/DM-Crypt inside the guest OS with keys in Key Vault. Microsoft now recommends encryption at host for new deployments."],["What protections does Trusted launch provide?","Secure boot to allow only signed boot components, a virtual TPM for measured boot and key storage, and boot integrity monitoring through Defender for Cloud."],["Why disable the admin user on Azure Container Registry?","The admin user is a single shared username and password with full push/pull rights. Using Entra identities (managed identities with AcrPull) gives per-identity, auditable, least-privilege access."],["What does an AI Gateway in API Management add in front of a model endpoint?","Central authentication (for example managed identity to the backend instead of distributing API keys), token-based rate limits and quotas per consumer, load balancing across deployments, and logging of prompts, tokens and usage for monitoring."],["What kind of threats do Defender for AI services alert on?","Threats to generative AI apps such as prompt injection and jailbreak attempts, sensitive data leakage, credential theft attempts and abnormal usage, using signals such as Azure AI Content Safety Prompt Shields."]],"4":[["What is the difference between foundational CSPM and Defender CSPM?","Foundational CSPM is free: secure score, recommendations, asset inventory and the Microsoft cloud security benchmark. Defender CSPM is paid and adds attack path analysis, cloud security explorer, agentless scanning, data-aware posture, governance rules and more compliance standards."],["When should you use an automation rule vs a playbook in Sentinel?","Automation rules handle simple incident triage inside Sentinel: assign an owner, change severity or status, add tags, suppress, or call a playbook. Playbooks (Logic Apps) perform external or multi-step actions such as posting to Teams, opening a ticket or disabling a user."],["How is secure score calculated?","Each security control has a maximum score. You earn its points only when all resources in scope are healthy for every recommendation in that control, so partial fixes earn partial credit proportionally to healthy resources."],["How do you collect Linux Syslog or CEF logs into Sentinel today?","Install the Azure Monitor Agent on a Linux forwarder (or the source), create a data collection rule selecting facilities and severities, and use the Syslog via AMA or CEF via AMA connector. The legacy Log Analytics agent is retired."],["What are Security Copilot SCUs and why do they matter?","Security Compute Units are the provisioned capacity that Security Copilot prompts and agents consume. You provision them (and optionally overage) in Azure; too few SCUs throttles usage and extra SCUs cost money every hour."],["What does attack path analysis show?","Chains of exploitable weaknesses, for example an internet-exposed VM with a high-severity vulnerability and a managed identity that can read a storage account with sensitive data, so you can fix the choke point first."]]},"qCount":90,"hasLessons":true,"hasLessonsEs":true,"hasPbqs":true});
